WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.880
Around 5-10% of all children are born with&nbsp;
dyslexia. In school they tend to read slowly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:12.640
have difficulty understanding simple texts, or&nbsp;
make a lot of spelling mistakes when writing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.200
Now while they are as intelligent as others,&nbsp;
having received years of instruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.760
doesn’t change the fact that their brains&nbsp;
struggle organizing symbols in their heads.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.240
Minds of children with dyslexia&nbsp;
may switch numbers when reading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.920
swap letters as they appear, or skip words&nbsp;
when writing. Some see their hand-written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:40.080
letters backwards and others struggle with grammar&nbsp;
rules — spelling correctly is often impossible.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.200
Before we learn what to do about that, we&nbsp;
can hear the true story of a dyslexic boy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:57.840
who overcame his weakness, used his disability to&nbsp;
an advantage, and became a self-made billionaire.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:04.480
Once upon a time there was a boy named&nbsp;
Ingvar. He grew up on a small farm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:10.160
Ingvar’s grandfather died after realizing he&nbsp;
could no longer afford to pay the mortgage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.160
But the grandmother’s dedication&nbsp;
and hard work saved the farm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:20.000
and the boy learned that perseverance can lead&nbsp;
to a better life — and a better life he wanted.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.920
But when Ingvar started school,&nbsp;
he struggled and at one point his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.960
father told him he would never amount to&nbsp;
anything. What nobody knew back then was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:36.480
Ingvar had dyslexia - inherited from his&nbsp;
ancestors just like the color of his hair.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:45.280
Regardless, he didn’t let his problems get him&nbsp;
down and at age 6, Ingvar started selling single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:51.040
matches to his neighbors. Soon after, he asked&nbsp;
his aunt in Stockholm to send him matches from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:57.840
the wholesale market. Ingvar then split the box&nbsp;
into smaller packages and resold them at a greater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:04.320
profit. Seeking his father’s support, the young&nbsp;
entrepreneur soon reached an entirely new level.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.760
By the age of 10, Ingvar would cross the&nbsp;
neighborhood on his bicycle, selling fish, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:18.880
pencils. At 14, he sold belts, watches and wallets&nbsp;
stocked under his bed at his boarding school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:26.240
In 1943, when he was 17 years old, his father&nbsp;
helped him to register his company. He named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.080
it after himself Ingvar Kamprad, plus the&nbsp;
initials from the farm and village he grew up in.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.200
Expanding his line of furniture, Ingvar&nbsp;
soon had trouble dealing with the product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:45.120
codes.To help him overcome his weakness, he&nbsp;
created a system where he, instead of numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:52.320
gave names to each object: Tables were named&nbsp;
after places, garden chairs after islands. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:59.200
Ingvar was able to remember and visualize each&nbsp;
product. By making shopping memorable, Ikea became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:05.200
the world's largest furniture company and Ingvar&nbsp;
Kamprad one of the richest men in the world.&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:10.160
But not all children are as lucky as our&nbsp;
protagonist and some need our support.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.360
To help children with dyslexia we can&nbsp;
strengthen their speech perception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.800
which children begin to form&nbsp;
at about six months old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.560
when they start to pay attention to the sounds&nbsp;
of spoken language. This usually happens as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:32.320
focus on the movements of their parents'&nbsp;
lips to link visuals with auditory input.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:38.480
Lip reading is important because our mind usually&nbsp;
prioritizes what we see over what we hear.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.760
Children who are raised with little to&nbsp;
no exposure to one-on-one conversations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.520
can have problems connecting auditory inputs&nbsp;
with lip and tongue movements. Some may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.880
not even realize that it takes a completely&nbsp;
different mouth, tongue and lip movement to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:03.520
produce letters as similar as B and D. They&nbsp;
lack what’s called phonological awareness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.360
And if they are also dyslexic,&nbsp;
they have it especially hard.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.640
Practicing phonological awareness can help&nbsp;
children train their minds to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.400
their own mouth movements, sounds and word&nbsp;
structures. Hence, professional therapists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.840
often focus first on the basics, such&nbsp;
as speaking and listening. Only later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.720
do they move on to the more complex forms of&nbsp;
communication such as reading and writing.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.600
By the way, in Finland, children&nbsp;
learn reading at age 6 or 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:44.400
and only 4% are diagnosed with dyslexia.&nbsp;
Kids in the UK start school at age 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:51.600
but the National Health Service estimates 10%&nbsp;
to be dyslexic. Later, Finish students rank top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.400
as the world's most literate people, while&nbsp;
British pupils rank somewhere in the middle.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:04.640
What are your thoughts about and experiences&nbsp;
with dyslexia? Did your teachers punish kids for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.840
making spelling mistakes or did they try to help&nbsp;
them? Share your thoughts in the comments below.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.840
To experience phonological awareness&nbsp;
yourself, do the famous McGurk Effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.200
experiment. It demonstrates how our&nbsp;
visual perception beats our auditory one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:37.840
and shows the importance of one-on-one&nbsp;
conversations. Links are in the descriptions.

